WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
GPU cost controls need ongoing operation.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:11.200
A useful dashboard connects spend to workload purpose, owner, environment, utilization, job success rate, failure rate, idle time, queue time, and trend.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.600
Spend alone says what happened.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.000
Spend with context helps explain why.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.800
Anomaly alerts help catch unexpected usage early.

6
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:29.600
A forgotten notebook, runaway retry loop, sudden inference traffic change, failed cleanup, or unusual data transfer pattern should be visible before it becomes a major budget problem.

7
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.800
Alerts should route to owners who can act.

8
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.400
Forecasting is more useful when it includes planned work.

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:42.400
Experiments, launches, evaluations, seasonal traffic, model refreshes, and migration projects can all change GPU demand.

10
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.000
Forecasts should combine historical spend with known upcoming events.

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.600
Showback or chargeback can improve accountability when used constructively.

12
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.400
The goal is not to shame teams.

13
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:59.600
The goal is to help teams understand the cost of their choices and compare spend against business value.

14
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.400
Regular cost reviews turn data into decisions.

15
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:09.200
A review may lead to resizing, rescheduling, cleanup, caching, model optimization, policy changes, or retiring a workload.

16
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.200
The best reviews produce concrete engineering actions, not just reports.

17
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.400
Reviews are most effective when they include both finance context and engineering context.

18
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.200
The cost chart shows where money went.

19
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.800
The engineering discussion explains whether that spend created value and what should change next.

20
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:36.000
A recurring review also helps teams notice patterns, such as repeated failed jobs, unused datasets, or inference capacity that no longer matches demand.
